  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"></math>In the third period, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Al</mi></math>&nbsp;comes after <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Mg</mi></math>&nbsp;and before <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Si</mi></math>. Since in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Mg</mi></math>&nbsp;(<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mn>6</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>3</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math> ), the last electron is to be lost from a fully filled <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math>-orbital while in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Al</mi></math>&nbsp;(<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>ls</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mn>6</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>3</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>3</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo></math>) it is to be lost from a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi></math>-orbital, therefore, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mo>&#8710;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Al</mi></math>&nbsp;is lower than that of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Mg</mi></math>&nbsp;(<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>737</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kJ</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>mol</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>). Further,&nbsp;nuclear charge increases in going from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Na</mi></math>&nbsp;to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Al</mi></math>, therefore <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mo>&#8710;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Al</mi></math>&nbsp;is higher than that of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Na</mi></math>(<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>496</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kJ</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>mol</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>) but lower than that of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Mg</mi></math>&nbsp;(<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>737</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kJ</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>mol</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>). Therefore, the appropriate value of ionisation potential of&nbsp; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Al</mi></math>&nbsp;is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>577</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kJ</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>mol</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>.</p></blockquote>",
